hrough the centre of mass. Estimate its position from the shape and mass distribution, then use weight times its perpendicular distance from the elbow.","The muscle balances that torque using its much shorter lever arm. Read distances from the diagram scale and convert centimetres to metres."]},"duplicate-74084147ddc54755":{"hint":"Use the rotational counterparts of force, mass and acceleration. Is the torque constant?","notes":["Use net torque τ = Iα. With constant angular acceleration, ω = ω₀ + αt and θ = ω₀t + ½αt².","Use radians and the moment of inertia about the stated axis. Moment of inertia depends on how mass is distributed, not only the total mass.","For “show that”, start from the supplied data and display the intermediate steps. Do not substitute the result you are asked to show as an assumption."]},"duplicate-2464cda8db943e32":{"hint":"Try listing the known motion quantities first. Which constant-acceleration equation leaves only the quantity you need?","notes":["For constant acceleration, use v = u + at, s = ut + ½at² or v² = u² + 2as. Choose the equation that avoids an unknown you do not need.","Choose one positive direction and keep the signs of displacement, velocity and acceleration consistent. Speed is the magnitude of velocity.","“From rest” sets the initial velocity to zero. It does not say that the acceleration is zero."]},"duplicate-9237ada202cd11da":{"hint":"Check whether there is an external torque. If it is negligible, compare angular momentum in the two states.","notes":["For rotation about a fixed axis, L = Iω. With negligible external torque, I_initial ω_initial = I_final ω_final.","Rotational kinetic energy need not remain constant when the mass distribution changes; internal work can change ½Iω²."]},"duplicate-50060ab0dcd70a32":{"hint":"Decide whether the question asks for energy over an interval or the rate of transfer at one instant.","notes":["Average power is energy transferred / time. Instantaneous mechanical power is P = Fv cos θ.","With a constant driving force, power still changes when speed changes. With constant power, driving force decreases as speed increases."]},"duplicate-8ce1913cb4529587":{"hint":"Use the rotational counterparts of force, mass and acceleration. Is the torque constant?","notes":["Use net torque τ = Iα. With constant angular acceleration, ω = ω₀ + αt and θ = ω₀t + ½αt².","Use radians and the moment of inertia about the stated axis. Moment of inertia depends on how mass is distributed, not only the total mass.","For “show that”, start from the supplied data and display the intermediate steps. Do not substitute the result you are asked to show as an assumption."]}}},"answers":{"entries":{"ibd-9f8859c3c69ea414-q8:whole":{"status":"editorial-solution","text":"Conservation of energy gives mgh = ½mv² + ½Iω². Rolling without slipping gives ω = v/r. Substituting I = 2mr²/5 gives mgh = ½mv² + ⅕mv² = 7mv²/10, hence v = √(10gh/7)."}}},"presentation":{"practice/supplement/sup-064316255265a882-6-a-eefdc87da31bc146.webp":{"crop":[0,4,1091,591],"size":[1091,758]},"practice/supplement/sup-064316255265a882-6-b.i-bb618a044708d15f.webp":{"crop":[0,4,1091,287],"size":[1091,310]},"practice/supplement/sup-064316255265a882-6-answer-b.i-2cdc00e566274e22.webp":{"crop":[0,0,1584,524],"size":[1584,649]},"practice/supplement/sup-064316255265a882-6-answer-b.i-3a4ad0d48b0695b2.webp":{"crop":[0,76,1584,41],"size":[1584,117],"masks":[[33,27,232,32]]},"practice/supplement/sup-064316255265a882-6-b.ii-a674d3d73ba89b4a.webp":{"crop":[0,4,1091,287],"size":[1091,310]},"practice/supplement/sup-064316255265a882-6-c-f04590ae92f77e28.webp":{"crop":[0,3,1091,388],"size":[1091,568]},"practice/supplement/sup-064316255265a882-6-answer-c-f54bfc46d7ee7895.webp":{"crop":[0,0,1584,50],"size":[1584,405]},"practice/supplement/sup-064316255265a882-6-answer-c-f1fcfc0b5ff7b7a9.webp":{"crop":[0,48,1584,42],"size":[1584,90]},"practice/supplement/sup-064316255265a882-6-stem-df6d3e2f3368f457.webp":{"crop":[0,4,1091,584],"siz